.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fIlibmpq\fP library creates and reads MPQ v1 and v2 archives. It provides
archive and file metadata queries, filename lookup, decryption, decompression,
and file extraction. Supported codecs include Huffman, zlib, bzip2, SPARSE,
PKWARE implosion, WAVE ADPCM, and the exclusive MPQ v2+ LZMA method.
MPQE wraps an MPQ archive in an encrypted byte stream; it is not a separate
MPQ format version.
.SS "Reading Archives and Files"
Open ordinary archives with \fBlibmpq__archive_open\fP(), or MPQE streams
with \fBlibmpq__archive_open_mpqe\fP() and a caller-supplied authentication
code. Resolve a member with \fBlibmpq__file_number\fP(), then read its
logical uncompressed contents with \fBlibmpq__file_read\fP().
Close archive handles with \fBlibmpq__archive_close\fP().
.PP
The file-stream API provides incremental, seekable logical-member access as
an alternative to complete-file \fBlibmpq__file_read\fP().
.PP
\fBlibmpq__file_flags\fP() reports stored block-table flags, while the file
size and block-count queries describe the member's storage. Block APIs
provide individual sector reads, packed and unpacked sizes, and the actual
stored compression method. A compressed file may contain raw fallback
sectors; \fBlibmpq__block_compression\fP() distinguishes them.
.SS "Creating Archives and Writing Files"
\fBlibmpq__archive_create\fP() creates a seekable MPQ v1 or v2 archive.
Creation options select the archive version, sector size, fixed file-table
capacity, and optional generated (listfile) and (attributes) entries.
.PP
Use \fBlibmpq__archive_add_data\fP() for an in-memory source or
\fBlibmpq__archive_add_path\fP() for a filesystem-path source.
For streaming input, \fBlibmpq__writer_begin\fP() returns an mpq_writer_s
handle. Supply bytes with \fBlibmpq__writer_write\fP(), optionally set a
FILETIME with \fBlibmpq__writer_timestamp\fP(), and complete the member
with \fBlibmpq__writer_finish\fP(). Finish consumes the writer handle
regardless of its result.
.PP
Closing a writer archive finalizes the listfile, attributes, then the hash
and block tables. Archive close consumes reader and writer archives even when
finalization or stream close reports an error.
.PP
\fBlibmpq__archive_create_mpqe\fP() creates an MPQE-wrapped archive through
a private temporary plaintext MPQ in the destination directory. The
plaintext temporary file is owner-only; final MPQE permissions follow the
caller's umask. Cleanup is best effort, and abrupt termination can leave
plaintext temporary files behind. Existing MPQE archives cannot be modified
in place; encrypted random-access writing is not implemented.
.SS "Compression Selection and Compatibility"
File options select raw, compressed, imploded, encrypted, or single-unit
storage. Compression selections may differ for the first and later sectors.
Only size-reducing compression is stored; other sectors fall back to raw data.
.PP
Writer compression defaults to STANDARD for interoperability. Set
LIBMPQ_ARCHIVE_CREATE_COMPRESSION_EXTENDED in the creation flags to allow
additional implemented combinations such as standalone Huffman in MPQ v2.
EXTENDED output may be less interoperable with other MPQ implementations.
Readers remain permissive and do not use this setting. Query whether a
selection is allowed with \fBlibmpq__archive_compression_allowed\fP().
.PP
For MPQ v2+ creation, LIBMPQ_COMPRESSION_LZMA is an exclusive API selector
that serializes as method 0x12. It is not a multi-compression bit; 0x12
retains its legacy bzip2-plus-zlib interpretation in MPQ v1 archives.
.PP
LIBMPQ_COMPRESSION_SPARSE (0x20) selects lossless zero-run compression.
MPQ v1 retains normal compression-mask semantics under either policy,
including valid combinations such as SPARSE with Huffman (0x21) or
PKWARE (0x28). In MPQ v2+, STANDARD allows SPARSE alone (0x20), SPARSE
with zlib (0x22), and SPARSE with bzip2 (0x30); EXTENDED permits the
broader valid lossless combinations. Neither policy allows SPARSE with
WAVE ADPCM. SPARSE extraction preserves the original bytes and text encoding.
.SS "Optional File Attributes"
The optional (attributes) file is supported for reading and creation in
MPQ v1 and v2, including MPQE. Its payload version 100 is independent of
the archive format version.
.PP
\fBlibmpq__archive_attributes\fP() queries the array-presence flags.
\fBlibmpq__file_attributes\fP() returns available per-file CRC32, FILETIME,
MD5, and patch-bit values. Loading is lazy: missing or malformed optional
metadata does not prevent ordinary extraction, while explicit queries return
the corresponding error. Availability flags distinguish zero from missing
values. These queries do not verify checksums.
.PP
Enable arrays through options.attributes using LIBMPQ_ATTRIBUTE_CRC32,
LIBMPQ_ATTRIBUTE_FILETIME, LIBMPQ_ATTRIBUTE_MD5, and LIBMPQ_ATTRIBUTE_PATCH_BIT.
Zero disables generation. Any nonzero combination creates one (attributes)
file and consumes one reserved file slot. Unknown attribute bits are rejected.
.PP
Generated CRC32 and MD5 values cover source bytes before compression; lossy
ADPCM output may differ and is not automatically compared. FILETIME defaults
to zero and can be set explicitly through
\fBlibmpq__writer_timestamp\fP() before file completion.
Path addition does not import filesystem timestamps. Generated patch bits
are zero; patch creation and application remain unsupported. Unused rows
and the attributes entry itself have zero values.
.SS "Checksum Verification"
Complete lossless \fBlibmpq__file_read\fP() calls automatically compare
available file CRC32 and MD5 values after decoding the full logical member.
Malformed optional metadata and lossy ADPCM members skip automatic comparison.
Complete reads also compare usable sector Adler-32 values over decrypted
packed sectors before decompression, including lossy ADPCM. Malformed optional
sector tables are skipped during ordinary extraction. FILETIME and PATCH_BIT
remain metadata only.
\fBlibmpq__file_verify\fP() compares requested, available file CRC32 and MD5
values against complete extracted contents. LIBMPQ_VERIFY_FILE_CRC32 and
LIBMPQ_VERIFY_FILE_MD5 select these checks. LIBMPQ_VERIFY_SECTOR_CRC checks
Adler-32 over decrypted packed sectors before decompression, using the
optional sector checksum table. LIBMPQ_VERIFY_ALL selects all three.
.PP
Checksum mismatches return success with the corresponding LIBMPQ_VERIFY_*
bits, a subset of the requested flags. Operational errors return negative
codes and leave the mismatch mask zero. File checksum requests require
attributes; an absent (attributes) file returns LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST.
Sector-only requests do not require attributes. Unavailable row values or
sector tables are skipped; single-unit files have no sector checksum table.
A clear mismatch bit means matched or skipped, not necessarily verified.
.PP
\fBlibmpq__block_verify\fP() verifies one sector and returns its stored
Adler-32 and either zero or LIBMPQ_VERIFY_SECTOR_CRC mismatch bits.
Unlike file verification, an absent table or unused zero/all-ones entry
returns LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST. Both outputs remain zero on operational errors.
.PP
For creation, LIBMPQ_FILE_FLAG_SECTOR_CRC enables sector Adler-32 tables for
sectorized COMPRESS and IMPLODE files, including encrypted files. It is
ignored for empty, raw, or single-unit files. The checksum table is not
encrypted and uses zlib compression only when smaller. Generation is opt-in
and does not enable implicit verification.
.SS "Concurrency and Archive Lifetime"
An archive handle is not safe for concurrent operations. Use
\fBlibmpq__archive_clone\fP() to create one independent reader per worker.
Cloning reopens the original backing path and reparses its metadata; it is
not snapshot semantics. The path must remain readable and unchanged while
clones are created or used.
